<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta
  http-equiv="X-UA-Compatible"
  content="IE=edge"
>
<meta
  name="viewport"
  content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"
>
<meta
  name="view-transition"
  content="same-origin"
>
{%- comment -%}
  No `theme-color` meta tag: it shipped with `content=""`, which is not a valid
  colour, so browsers ignored it on every page render. Nothing usable is in
  Liquid scope here either. The colour schemes render after this snippet and
  emit CSS custom properties, not a Liquid-readable value. Re-adding it means
  sourcing a literal colour, not filling this tag back in blank.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign og_title = page_title | default: shop.name
  assign og_url = canonical_url | default: request.origin
  assign og_type = 'website'

  # Neither /search nor /collections has an Admin-editable SEO description, and
  # Shopify fills `page_description` on both from the shop's own meta
  # description. That put one identical description on three URLs (/, /search,
  # /collections), which is the duplicate-description class the July 2026 audit
  # closed on products. The homepage keeps the shop description, because it is
  # the page that description is about; the other two are handled here.
  #
  # /search is noindexed below, so it needs no description at all. /collections
  # is indexable and gets its own line. Change the copy in the locale files, not
  # here, and remember `locales/it.json` and `locales/ro.json` mirror it.
  assign seo_description = page_description
  if request.page_type == 'search'
    assign seo_description = ''
  elsif request.page_type == 'list-collections'
    assign seo_description = 'content.collections_list_meta_description' | t
  endif

  # og:description keeps the old fallback chain so a social card is never blank,
  # even on the pages that deliberately emit no meta description.
  assign og_description = seo_description | default: shop.description | default: shop.name

  if request.page_type == 'product'
    assign og_type = 'product'
  elsif request.page_type == 'article'
    assign og_type = 'article'
  elsif request.page_type == 'password'
    assign og_url = request.origin
  endif
%}

<meta
  property="og:site_name"
  content="{{ shop.name }}"
>
<meta
  property="og:url"
  content="{{ og_url }}"
>
<meta
  property="og:title"
  content="{{ og_title | escape }}"
>
<meta
  property="og:type"
  content="{{ og_type }}"
>
<meta
  property="og:description"
  content="{{ og_description | escape }}"
>

{%- if page_image -%}
  <meta
    property="og:image"
    content="https:{{ page_image | image_url }}"
  >
  <meta
    property="og:image:secure_url"
    content="https:{{ page_image | image_url }}"
  >
  <meta
    property="og:image:width"
    content="{{ page_image.width }}"
  >
  <meta
    property="og:image:height"
    content="{{ page_image.height }}"
  >
{%- endif -%}

{%- if request.page_type == 'product' -%}
  <meta
    property="og:price:amount"
    content="{{ product.price | money_without_currency | strip_html }}"
  >
  <meta
    property="og:price:currency"
    content="{{ cart.currency.iso_code }}"
  >
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  No `twitter:site`. The tag used to be emitted here from
  `settings.social_twitter_link`, a setting that never existed in
  `settings_schema.json`, so the branch was unreachable. It was also wrong: the
  handle was extracted with `split: 'twitter.com/'`, which does not match an
  `x.com` URL, so an X profile would have rendered
  `content="@https://x.com/handle"` rather than `@handle`.

  To restore it, add the setting back AND parse the handle from both hosts. Do not
  add the setting alone; that reactivates the broken split.
{%- endcomment -%}
<meta
  name="twitter:card"
  content="summary_large_image"
>
<meta
  name="twitter:title"
  content="{{ og_title | escape }}"
>
<meta
  name="twitter:description"
  content="{{ og_description | escape }}"
>

<title>
  {{ page_title }}
  {%- if current_tags %} &ndash; tagged "{{ current_tags | join: ', ' }}"{% endif -%}
  {%- if current_page != 1 %} &ndash; Page {{ current_page }}{% endif -%}
  {%- unless page_title contains shop.name %} &ndash; {{ shop.name }}{% endunless -%}
</title>

{%- comment -%}
  An empty blog is a thin-content signal, so a blog listing with no articles is
  kept out of the index. The guard is the article count, not a hardcoded handle,
  so this is self-clearing: publish the first article and the tag stops being
  emitted on its own. Do not replace it with an unconditional noindex on the
  blog page type; that would survive publishing and silently bury real posts.

  `follow` is deliberate: the page still links into the catalogue, and those
  links should keep passing through. Article pages are never touched, since an
  article existing means there is content to index.

  Shopify's generated sitemap still lists the blog, so `scripts/seo-review`
  reports this as `robots-noindex` (crawl) and `surface-noindex` (anonymous
  sweep); both carry an accepted-risks entry keyed to `/blogs/news` that goes
  away with the tag.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if request.page_type == 'blog' and blog.articles_count == 0 -%}
  <meta
    name="robots"
    content="noindex, follow"
  >
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Internal search result pages are noindexed for the ordinary reason: they are
  generated per query, so an indexable /search mints an unbounded set of thin,
  near-duplicate URLs out of whatever anyone happens to type. `follow` again, so
  the product links on a results page keep passing through.

  Kept as its own guard rather than folded into the blog condition above so each
  tag stays next to the reasoning for it. `search` is already in `NOINDEX_OK` in
  `scripts/seo-review/lib/checks.mjs`, so unlike the blog tag this one needs no
  accepted-risks entry.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if request.page_type == 'search' -%}
  <meta
    name="robots"
    content="noindex, follow"
  >
{%- endif -%}

<link
  rel="canonical"
  href="{{ canonical_url }}"
>

{% if seo_description != blank %}
  <meta
    name="description"
    content="{{ seo_description | escape }}"
  >
{% endif %}
